nodecode-markifact is a rewrite of markifact's markifact/markifact-mcp:

  https://github.com/markifact/markifact-mcp
  commit 9a873931b6138970888d2a9102a8037dbbd174a5
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Markifact (LICENSE)

The rewritten plugin is plugins/hermes/markifact of that commit, a portable Agent
Plugins v1 package: plugin.json, mcp.json and ten skills. It ships no code. The
plugin points an MCP client at https://api.markifact.com/mcp (streamable HTTP,
OAuth 2.1 with PKCE and dynamic client registration, no API keys) and teaches
the model a performance-marketing trade. The rewrite is that client in Common
Lisp: INITIALIZE, TOOLS/LIST and TOOLS/CALL over the same single endpoint, the
same eight MCP tools (find_operations, get_operation_inputs, run_operation,
run_write_operation, list_connections, get_file_url, read_file, upload_media),
and a sign-in that walks the same OAuth 2.1 flow from /markifact and keeps the
tokens where Nodecode keeps its other ones. The plugin's operating protocol
(discover, inspect, resolve the account, run, verify; confirm every write) and
its seven workflow skills are carried over as the (help :markifact) topic and
as /markifact workflow, condensed from their prose to their steps.

What was left out, and why: the MCP runtime itself, because a Nodecode cell is
Lisp and the server speaks a documented HTTP protocol, so the eight tools are
reached through two of the model's own (markifact_tools lists them, and
markifact_call calls one by name) instead of eight hand-typed wrappers whose
schemas only the server knows. The seven skills are no longer separate slash
commands, since Nodecode's catalog names commands one word at a time; /markifact
workflow NAME prints the same steps. The skill text is condensed rather than
copied, because the skills are generated in the original repository from a
shared/ directory that is not part of the plugin folder. LICENSE is that
repository's LICENSE, unchanged.
